cap     The GCC 1.39 C compiler pass.  It reports its version; the driver
cap     runs it as part of a compile.
try     gcc_cc1 -version
run     gcc_cc1 -version
wait    8

shot    gcc_cc1plus
cap     The GCC 1.x C++ compiler pass (version 1.37.1), reporting its
cap     version.
try     gcc_cc1plus -version
run     gcc_cc1plus -version
wait    8

shot    gcc_cccp
cap     The GCC 1.39 preprocessor pass, expanding a small C file.  The
cap     `# 1 "..."' line marks the source; there are no macros to expand
cap     here.
try     gcc_cccp DOC/samples/hello.c tmp/hello.i
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     gcc_cccp DOC/samples/hello.c tmp/hello.i
wait    8
run     cat tmp/hello.i
wait    5

shot    gcc_collect
fresh
cap     collect2 again -- the same global-constructor collector as the
cap     GCC2 directory's, reached here under the GCC 1.39 name.  Given an
cap     object with one global object whose class has a constructor, it
cap     writes the table naming that constructor, _GLOBAL_$I$main, into
cap     the file -o names.  The object is made by `gpp -c', which
cap     assembles with your own OS-9's r68, resident.
cap     This 1991 build tests its scan pointer before it sets it, so
cap     where its memory lands can leave the table empty (`dc.l 0');
cap     gcc272_collect is built from the source that fixes it.
try     gcc_collect -o ctors.a greet.r; cat ctors.a
run     load /h1/CMDS/r68
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/CLGC; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CLGC; builtin cd /dd/tmp/CLGC
run     printf "class Greeter {\rpublic:\r  Greeter() { n = 1; }\r  int n;\r};\rGreeter hello;\rint main()\r{\r  return hello.n;\r}\r" > greet.cc
run     /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gpp -c greet.cc -o greet.r > /nil 2>/nil
wait    12
run     clear
wait    1
run     gcc_collect -o ctors.a greet.r
wait    8
run     cat ctors.a
wait    3

shot    gcc_cc2
cap     The GCC 2.x C compiler pass installed on the command path under
cap     this name, so the `gcc2' driver can fork it.  Run alone it reports
cap     its version and options.
try     gcc_cc2 -version
run     gcc_cc2 -version
wait    8

shot    gcc_cccp2
cap     The GCC 2.x preprocessor installed on the command path under the
cap     name the `gcc2' driver forks it by.  It reports its version and
cap     the two directories it searches for headers.
try     gcc_cccp2 -version
run     gcc_cccp2 -version
wait    8

shot    gpp_cc1plus
cap     The GCC C++ compiler pass installed on the command path under the
cap     name the `gpp' driver forks it by.  It reports its version.
try     gpp_cc1plus -version
run     gpp_cc1plus -version
wait    8

shot    gpp_cccp
cap     The GCC 2.x preprocessor under the name the `gpp' driver forks it
cap     by -- the same program as `gcc_cccp2'.  It reports its version and
cap     its header search path.
try     gpp_cccp -version
run     gpp_cccp -version
wait    8

shot    gpp_collect
fresh
cap     collect2 under the name the `gpp' driver forks it by -- the same
cap     collector as the others.  Given an object with one global object
cap     whose class has a constructor, it writes the table naming that
cap     constructor, _GLOBAL_$I$main, into the file -o names.  The object
cap     is made by `gpp -c', which assembles with your own OS-9's r68,
cap     resident.
cap     This 1991 build tests its scan pointer before it sets it, so
cap     where its memory lands can leave the table empty (`dc.l 0');
cap     gcc272_collect is built from the source that fixes it.
try     gpp_collect -o ctors.a greet.r; cat ctors.a
run     load /h1/CMDS/r68
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/CLGP; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CLGP; builtin cd /dd/tmp/CLGP
run     printf "class Greeter {\rpublic:\r  Greeter() { n = 1; }\r  int n;\r};\rGreeter hello;\rint main()\r{\r  return hello.n;\r}\r" > greet.cc
run     /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gpp -c greet.cc -o greet.r > /nil 2>/nil
wait    12
run     clear
wait    1
run     gpp_collect -o ctors.a greet.r
wait    8
run     cat ctors.a
wait    3

shot    gmake
cap     GNU make, running a rule from a shipped makefile: it copies one
cap     file to another and reports the file it made.
try     gmake -f DOC/make/demo.mk greeting
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     rm -f tmp/greeting
wait    4
run     gmake -v
wait    5
run     gmake -f DOC/make/demo.mk greeting
wait    10
run     ls -l tmp/greeting
wait    6

shot    dmake
cap     dmake, a parallel make, on the same rule -- its own makefile
cap     dialect still reads the shipped makefile and builds the target.
try     dmake -f DOC/make/demo.mk greeting
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     rm -f tmp/greeting
wait    4
run     dmake -V
wait    5
run     dmake -f DOC/make/demo.mk greeting
wait    10
run     ls -l tmp/greeting
wait    6

shot    m4
cap     m4, the macro processor.  `define' names a macro with a parameter,
cap     and each later use of it is expanded -- here `greet(reader)'
cap     becomes a short welcome with the name filled in -- and `syscmd'
cap     runs a command where it stands, through your own OS-9's `shell',
cap     which SYS/login loads from /h1; without it syscmd is silent.
try     m4 tmp/m.m4
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     echo "define(greet,hi \0441 -- welcome)" > tmp/m.m4
wait    5
run     echo "greet(reader)" >> tmp/m.m4
wait    5
run     echo "syscmd(whoami)" >> tmp/m.m4
wait    5
run     cat tmp/m.m4
wait    5
run     m4 tmp/m.m4
wait    8

shot    m4_0.5
cap     A second build of the m4 macro processor.  It reads a macro
cap     definition and expands each use: `greet(world)' becomes
cap     `hello world'.
try     m4_0.5 tmp/BLGX/mm.m4
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/BLGX > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'define(greet,hello $1)greet(world)\r' > /dd/tmp/BLGX/mm.m4
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
run     m4_0.5 tmp/BLGX/mm.m4
wait    5

shot    make
cap     `make' building a target from a shipped makefile: it runs the
cap     rule's command and names the file it made.  Run again with the
cap     target already up to date, it does nothing and stays silent.
try     make -f DOC/make/demo.mk greeting
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     rm -f tmp/greeting
wait    5
run     make -f DOC/make/demo.mk greeting
wait    12
run     ls -l tmp/greeting
wait    8
run     make -f DOC/make/demo.mk greeting
wait    10

shot    makeinfo
cap     GNU makeinfo turns Texinfo source into Info files.  Its options
cap     take a leading `+', not the GNU-style `--', so `+version' answers
cap     where `--version' would not.
try     makeinfo +version
run     makeinfo +version
wait    6

shot    a2p
size    24 80
cap     a2p turns an awk program into a perl script.  count.awk adds up the
cap     words on each line and prints the line and word counts at the end;
cap     a2p writes the perl version, and perl runs it on the password file.
try     a2p count.awk > count.pl; perl count.pl password
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/A2PC > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/A2PC
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/A2PC
wait    1
run     cp /dd/SYS/password password; echo '{ w += NF } END { print NR, w }' > count.awk
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat count.awk
wait    2
run     a2p count.awk > count.pl
wait    6
run     perl count.pl password
wait    6

shot    p2c
size    36 80
cap     p2c translating Pascal into C, which is what it is for.  It writes
cap     `h.c' beside the source.  The warning is p2c's own and correct:
cap     nothing has told it what `writeln' is, because the Pascal run-time
cap     declarations live elsewhere and this one-line program does not ask
cap     for them.
try     p2c h.pas
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/P2C > /nil 2>/nil
run     echo "program hello; begin writeln('hi') end." > /dd/tmp/P2C/h.pas
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/P2C
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat h.pas
wait    4
run     p2c h.pas
wait    12
run     ls
wait    4
run     head -n 12 h.c
wait    8

shot    yacc
cap     yacc reading a grammar and writing `y.tab.c', the parser tables,
cap     into the data directory -- the two-rule grammar shown above.  It
cap     prints nothing on success; the listed file and its size confirm it
cap     finished.
try     yacc g.y
run     rm -f /dd/tmp/CMPA/g.y /dd/tmp/CMPA/y.tab.c; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CMPA > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf "%%token NUM\r%%%%\rexpr : expr NUM | NUM ;\r" > /dd/tmp/CMPA/g.y
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/CMPA
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat g.y
wait    4
run     yacc g.y
wait    15
run     ls -l y.tab.c
wait    6

shot    fact
super
size    50 80
cap     The Fortran example, end to end: the source, this disk's RTF/68K
cap     compiling it to 68000 assembly, your own OS-9's `r68' assembling
cap     that and the Fortran start-up code, `l68' linking both against its
cap     `sys.l', and the finished program printing 1! to 12!.  Twelve is as
cap     far as a 32-bit integer reaches -- 13! overflows.  r68, l68, sys.l
cap     and the `oskdefs.d' r68 reads come from /h1 and were put in place
cap     first, with the run-time library every RTF program wants: `load
cap     os9lib'.  l68 writes the program to your execution directory --
cap     here CMDS, which on this disk only the super-user may write, so
cap     this card runs as the super-user -- in place of the fact that
cap     ships, removed first, so what runs is the one just built.
try     rtf fact.f
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/FACT > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/FACT
wait    3
run     cp /dd/SRC/rtf/fact.f /dd/SRC/rtf/rtfstart.a /h1/DEFS/oskdefs.d /h1/LIB/sys.l /dd/tmp/FACT; rm -f /dd/CMDS/fact
wait    4
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib /h1/CMDS/r68 /h1/CMDS/l68
wait    5
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/FACT
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat fact.f
wait    4
run     rtf fact.f
wait    15
run     r68 -q fact.a -o=fact.r
wait    8
run     r68 -q rtfstart.a -o=rtfstart.r
wait    8
run     l68 rtfstart.r fact.r -l=sys.l -o=fact
wait    8
run     fact
wait    8

shot    rtf
cap     RTF/68K compiling Fortran-77: with its run-time library loaded
cap     first (`load os9lib'), `rtf' reads the source and writes 68k
cap     assembly beside it.  Assembling and linking that is `r68' and
cap     `l68', which come with your OS-9, so your system finishes the
cap     build.
try     rtf div.f
run     rm -f /dd/tmp/CMPA/div.f /dd/tmp/CMPA/div.a; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CMPA > /nil 2>/nil
wait    3
run     cp /dd/SRC/rtf/div.f /dd/tmp/CMPA/div.f
wait    3
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib
wait    5
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/CMPA
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     rtf div.f
wait    18
run     ls -l div.a
wait    6

shot    for
super
size    40 80
cap     The RTF Fortran compile driver: `for fact' compiles fact.f with
cap     `rtf', assembles the result with `r68' and deletes the assembly,
cap     printing each command as it runs it through `shell'.  Then `lnk
cap     fact' links it into a program and `fact' runs it: 1! to 12!.  The
cap     shell, r68, l68 and del are your own OS-9's, loaded from /h1 with
cap     the Fortran run-time, os9lib.  lnk installs into CMDS, which on
cap     this disk only the super-user may write, so this card runs as the
cap     super-user.  At bash `for' is also the loop keyword,
cap     so there it is `command for'; at the OS-9 shell just `for'.
try     command for fact; lnk fact; fact
os9     for fact; lnk fact; fact
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/FORA > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/FORA; cp /dd/SRC/rtf/fact.f /dd/tmp/FORA; cp /h1/LIB/sys.l /dd/LIB; rm -f /dd/CMDS/fact
wait    4
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib /h1/CMDS/shell /h1/CMDS/r68 /h1/CMDS/l68 /h1/CMDS/del
wait    5
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/FORA
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     command for fact
wait    15
run     lnk fact
wait    10
run     fact
wait    6

shot    lnk
super
cap     The RTF Fortran link driver: `lnk fact' links fact.r with the
cap     Fortran start-up code and Microware's `sys.l' into a program, and
cap     prints the `l68' command it runs.  It looks for both in LIB on /h0
cap     and writes the program to CMDS there, as its authors' systems were
cap     laid out; on this disk only the super-user may write CMDS, so this
cap     card runs as the super-user.  The start-up code,
cap     rtfstart.r, ships in LIB from the RTF archive; sys.l is your own
cap     OS-9's, copied into LIB from /h1.  fact was compiled with `for' and
cap     the fact that ships removed first, so the fact that runs is the one
cap     lnk just made.
try     lnk fact
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/LNKA > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/LNKA; cp /dd/SRC/rtf/fact.f /dd/tmp/LNKA; cp /h1/LIB/sys.l /dd/LIB; rm -f /dd/CMDS/fact
wait    4
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib /h1/CMDS/shell /h1/CMDS/r68 /h1/CMDS/l68 /h1/CMDS/del
wait    5
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/LNKA
wait    1
run     command for fact > /nil
wait    15
run     clear
wait    1
run     lnk fact
wait    10
run     fact
wait    6

shot    lnk.org
super
cap     The original build of `lnk', the RTF Fortran link driver.  It does
cap     the same job and names the start-up code `fstart.r' where lnk says
cap     `rtfstart.r' -- the same SRC/rtf/rtfstart.a, assembled under the
cap     other name into LIB on /h0, beside Microware's `sys.l'.  Set up as
cap     lnk's card is, it links fact.r without a word, and the program runs.
try     lnk.org fact
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/LNKB > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/LNKB /dd/LIB; cp /dd/SRC/rtf/fact.f /dd/SRC/rtf/rtfstart.a /h1/DEFS/oskdefs.d /dd/tmp/LNKB; cp /h1/LIB/sys.l /dd/LIB; rm -f /dd/CMDS/fact
wait    4
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib /h1/CMDS/shell /h1/CMDS/r68 /h1/CMDS/l68 /h1/CMDS/del
wait    5
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/LNKB
wait    1
run     r68 -q rtfstart.a -o=/dd/LIB/fstart.r
wait    5
run     command for fact > /nil
wait    15
run     clear
wait    1
run     lnk.org fact
wait    10
run     fact
wait    6

shot    v7make
size    24 80
cap     The make of Seventh Edition Unix: a makefile names a target, what it
cap     depends on and the commands to bring it up to date, and v7make runs
cap     those whose target is older than its dependencies.  It prints each
cap     command as it runs it, and hands it to `shell' -- your own OS-9's,
cap     which SYS/login loads from /h1.  Public domain.
try     v7make count
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
wait    3
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/VMK > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/VMK
wait    3
run     cp /dd/DOC/samples/jabber.txt /dd/tmp/VMK/poem; printf "count: poem\r\twc poem\r" > /dd/tmp/VMK/makefile
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/VMK
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat makefile
wait    3
run     v7make count
wait    6

shot    ltb
size    24 80
cap     Builds a lexical table: it reads a list of words and writes the hash
cap     table for them as C, ready to compile in.  This is what makes `proff'
cap     rebuildable -- the words are proff's own requests, from SRC/proff,
cap     and what comes out is the lextab.h and lextab.d its parser is
cap     compiled from, byte for byte as they ship.
try     ltb proffsym.new lextab
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/LTB > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/LTB
wait    3
run     cp /dd/SRC/proff/proffsym.new /dd/tmp/LTB
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/LTB
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     head -n 3 < proffsym.new
wait    3
run     ltb proffsym.new lextab
wait    6
run     ls -l
wait    4
run     head -n 4 < lextab.h
wait    4

shot    creadoc
size    28 90
cap     An early Fortran documentation generator: it walks the `.f' files
cap     in the data directory and copies each one's header comment into
cap     `creadoc.txt'.  It lists the directory through your own OS-9's
cap     `dir', and needs the Fortran run-time and a `shell' loaded too.
try     creadoc
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/CRD; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CRD; cp /dd/SRC/rtf/biory.f /dd/tmp/CRD/biory.f
wait    3
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib; load /h1/CMDS/shell; load /h1/CMDS/dir; load /h1/CMDS/del
wait    6
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/CRD
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     creadoc
wait    12
